{ Exercise. Try the questions in the book before reading this line. } 1. Ne5 f6 2. Nf3 { Illustrative line. The f-pawn is still on f7, so ...f6 is available. A black pawn on f6 attacks e5. This future possibility changes the square's stability. Ne5 f6 Nf3 shows the knight being driven away. The route back is legal, but White has spent time going to a square that Black can challenge. This is a comparison line, not a recommendation to repeat the maneuver. No. A temporary knight move can gain time or provoke a weakening pawn move in a richer position. Here the exercise asks whether e5 is a permanent pawn-proof outpost; it is not. Check the whole position before choosing the move. } *

