{ Exercise. Try the questions in the book before reading this line. } 1. Bf4 Nc6 { Illustrative line. White has added a pawn on c3. It stands directly on the bishop's route d2-c3-b4. No. The bishop cannot jump over its own pawn. The presence of an attractive square on b4 does not create a legal route to it. Bf4 is legal through e3. It places the bishop outside d2 and controls squares such as g5, although its own e5-pawn still blocks the diagonal toward d6. It is a modest improvement, not a claim that the bishop is suddenly unrestricted. } *
